      "writtenOn": "2026-10-19T16:43:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks, that\u0027s a fair point. I\u0027m planning to change the platform schema so that each endpoint behind a bridge becomes a record of its own rather than an entry in BridgedEndpoints, for example:\n\n    {\n        \"Type\": \"MCTPUSBDevice\", \"Name\": \"Bridge_0\", \"Board\": \"...\",\n        \"RootHubPosition\": 1, \"Port\": [1, 2],\n        \"Configuration\": 1, \"InterfaceNumber\": 0\n    },\n    {\n        \"Type\": \"MCTPBridgedEndpoint\", \"Name\": \"Device_0\", \"Board\": \"...\",\n        \"Bridge\": \"Bridge_0\", \"BridgePort\": 2, \"Address\": \"0x32\"\n    }\n\nThe bridge\u0027s routing table (Get Routing Table Entries in DSP0236) reports the port and physical address behind each EID it routes, so BridgePort and Address can pair a record with the EID its device was given, even when EIDs are assigned dynamically. The goal is for each bridged endpoint object to be associated with its EID through configured_by, so that a service such as SPDM or PLDM can find the EID of a device behind a bridge. Because of that I\u0027d rather not add BridgedEndpoints to this interface for now.\n\nWhat do you think? If this works for you, I\u0027ll update the platform schema and add documentation for it.",
      "parentUuid": "e8f1c3ae_b5b2a050",
      "revId": "1d2ff9ef898ba0eea891af6749786b9209710252",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
